{"id":"retrievalqa-triviaqa_odql_14010","question":"Write a detailed report, with sections, on Formula One team and driver changes announced for the end of 2026, based on what the searches found.","answers":[],"need":true,"passes":[{"stop":0,"gen":304,"calls":0,"err":""}],"raw":"\n\nNo more tool calls. My reply will be only the answer, or that I could not find it.\n</think>\n## Formula One Team and Driver Changes Announced for the End of 2026\n\nBased on the search results, here are the key announced changes:\n\n### Team Changes\n- **Haas Formula 1 Team**: Announced that **Esteban Ocon will leave Haas at the end of the 2026 season**. He joined Haas from Alpine for 2025 and had been with the team for two years. His replacement has not yet been announced.\n- **Audi (Hinwil-based operation)**: **Beat Zehnder, a long-serving team official, will leave Audi (Hinwil) at the end of December 2026**, bringing to a close a 39-year association as the team restructures ahead of the 2027 Formula 1 season.\n\n### Driver Changes\n- **Esteban Ocon**: Confirmed he is to leave Haas at the end of the 2026 campaign, following weeks of speculation over his future.\n\n### Additional Context\n- **Alpine**: The team has been unable to regain engine performance after a freeze introduced in 2022, which runs until the end of 2025. This relates to the engine situation for 2026, but no specific team withdrawal was announced in the snippets.\n\n**Note**: The search results provided specific announcements for Ocon's departure from Haas and Beat Zehnder's departure from Audi. No broader team-wide withdrawals for 2026 were found beyond these individual exits."}
{"id":"retrievalqa-realtimeqa_20231020_10","question":"Summarize in several paragraphs what happened on President Biden's wartime visit, and why it mattered.","answers":[],"need":true,"passes":[{"stop":0,"gen":201,"calls":0,"err":""}],"raw":"\n\nNo more tool calls. My reply will be only the answer, or that I could not find it.\n</think>\nOn November 4, 2026, President Joe Biden visited wartime Israel in Tel Aviv for a 7½-hour trip. He arrived amid the ongoing Israel–Hamas conflict and met with Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu, offering strong support for the Israeli people and urging restraint. During the visit, Biden announced a deal to secure limited humanitarian aid into Gaza from Egypt, potentially arriving by the end of the week, and he warned against allowing the conflict to escalate further. The visit mattered because it signaled a direct, high-level U.S. commitment to Israel’s security and humanitarian needs while also addressing the urgent need for aid to civilians trapped in Gaza. \n\n**Key points:**\n- **Location:** Tel Aviv, Israel.\n- **Duration:** 7½ hours.\n- **Context:** Ongoing war between Israel and Hamas.\n- **Outcome:** Announced humanitarian aid deal from Egypt and a warning against escalation.\n\n*Sources: AP News, The Star.*"}
{"id":"retrievalqa-realtimeqa_20231103_13","question":"Write a detailed report, with sections, on the eye drop recall: who issued the warning, which retailers and products, and what consumers should do.","answers":[],"need":true,"passes":[{"stop":0,"gen":478,"calls":0,"err":""}],"raw":"\n\nNo more tool calls. My reply will be only the answer, or that I could not find it.\n</think>\n# Eye Drop Recall Report (2026-11-04)\n\n## Who Issued the Warning\nThe **U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA)** issued the recall notice. It was reported by ABC News (GMA Wellness) and Yahoo, stating that Target, Rite Aid, and CVS are removing eye drop products from stores and websites due to potential bacterial contamination risk.\n\n## Affected Products and Retailers\nThe FDA recalled eye drop products sold at **Target, Rite Aid, and CVS** (and possibly others). The specific brand names mentioned include:\n- **Leader** (Target)\n- **Rugby** (Rite Aid)\n- **Velocity** (Target)\n- Also, other private-label brands like **KC Pharmaceuticals Inc.** produced products under multiple brand names.\n\nThe recall was due to a **lack of assurance of sterility** and potential risk of eye infections caused by bacterial contamination.\n\n## What Consumers Should Do\n- **Do not purchase** the recalled eye drop products from Target, Rite Aid, or CVS.\n- Check if your product is affected: look for brand names like Leader, Rugby, Velocity, or any private-label eye drops produced by KC Pharmaceuticals Inc.\n- Millions of bottles have been recalled across CVS, Walgreens, Kroger, HEB, Meijer, and other stores.\n- If you have a bottle, **do not use it** until you can confirm it is safe.\n- For more details, refer to the FDA recall notice and the articles from ABC News and Yahoo.\n\n## Summary Table\n\n| Aspect | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| **Issuing Agency** | U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) |\n| **Retailers/Stores** | Target, Rite Aid, CVS, Walgreens, Kroger, HEB, Meijer, and others |\n| **Products** | Eye drop products (brand names: Leader, Rugby, Velocity, etc.) |\n| **Reason** | Potential bacterial contamination leading to eye infections; lack of sterility assurance |\n| **Consumer Action** | Stop purchasing affected eye drops; check product branding; do not use recalled bottles |\n\n*Sources: ABC News (GMA/Wellness), Yahoo, Digital ABC Audio, USA Today, Newsweek.*"}
{"id":"retrievalqa-freshqa_406","question":"Write a detailed guide to the most recent Microsoft Patch Tuesday: the date, what it fixed, and what administrators should do.","answers":[],"need":true,"passes":[{"stop":0,"gen":361,"calls":0,"err":""}],"raw":"\n\nNo more tool calls. My reply will be only the answer, or that I could not find it.\n</think>\nBased on the search results, the most recent Microsoft Patch Tuesday is **November 10, 2026**. It addresses **1,411 vulnerabilities** (including 96 rated Critical, one actively exploited, and two publicly disclosed zero-days) across Windows, Microsoft Office, and Developer Tools. For Windows 11 versions 23H2 Enterprise and Education, this is the final update available; devices running these editions will no longer receive monthly security updates after this date.\n\nAdministrators should:\n- Deploy the latest patch (KB5126653, OS Build 22631.7652, etc.) to affected systems.\n- Prioritize the actively exploited vulnerability (CVE-2026-96951, first listed).\n- Upgrade to the latest Windows 11 version to stay protected.\n- Review the full CVE list (1461 total, 96 Critical) and follow Microsoft’s release notes for Hotpatch details.\n\nSources: \n- [Patch Tuesday November 2026: Date, Live Coverage, What to Expect](https://senserva.com/patch-tuesday-2026-11.html)  \n- [Microsoft October 2026 Patch Tuesday fixes 1,411 flaws, 3 zero-days](https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/microsoft/microsoft-october-2026-patch-tuesday-fixes-1-411-flaws-3-zero-days/)  \n- [KB5126653 – Windows 11 security update](https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/servicing/os/windows-11/2026/10/kb5126653-windows-11-23h2-security-update)  \n- [Patch Tuesday Tracker](https://patchtuesday.istwpro.com/)"}
{"id":"retrievalqa-freshqa_484","question":"Write a detailed profile, with sections, of the latest American Nobel laureate in Physics and the work that earned the prize.","answers":[],"need":true,"passes":[{"stop":1,"gen":1024,"calls":0,"err":""}],"raw":"\n\nNo more tool calls. My reply will be only the answer, or that I could not find it.\n</think>\n## Latest American Nobel Laureate in Physics (2026)\n\n**Name:** Francis Halzen  \n**Nationality:** American (implied by the context of the Nobel Prize being awarded to an American; the search results list him as the laureate, and the Nobel Prize in Physics 2026 press release and facts page are on NobelPrize.org, which lists laureates by name and often includes nationalities or affiliations; however, the exact nationality is not explicitly stated in the snippets I retrieved. I will note that the search results do not explicitly confirm \"American\" for Francis Halzen, but the question asks for the \"latest American Nobel laureate in Physics\". I need to verify if he is American. The search result [3] from Wikipedia might have info. Let me see if I can get more. I didn't fetch the Wikipedia page. I could have fetched it but the tool calls are exhausted. I need to answer from what I already have. The snippets mention \"The Nobel Prize in Physics 2026 was awarded to Francis Halzen\" and \"The Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences has decided to award...\" but not nationality. I might need to infer or state that I couldn't verify nationality from the snippets. However, the user asked for a detailed profile with sections, of the latest American Nobel laureate in Physics and the work that earned the prize. I have the work: \"for decisive contributions to the IceCube Neutrino Observatory and the discovery of high-energy neutrinos of astrophysical origin.\" I need more details: his background, contributions, etc. I might not have enough. But I should try to use the facts page URL. I could have fetched it but tool calls are done. I'll answer with what I have, noting limitations.\n\nLet me structure:\n\n- Introduction\n- Award details\n- Work that earned the prize\n- Contributions\n- Background (if known)\n- Impact\n\nBut I need sections. I'll create headings.\n\nGiven the snippets: \n- Nobel Prize in Physics 2026 was awarded to Francis Halzen \"for decisive contributions to the IceCube Neutrino Observatory and the discovery of high-energy neutrinos of astrophysical origin\" (from NobelPrize.org press release).\n- He laid the foundation for an entirely new type of astronomy (from NobelPrize.org summary).\n- He has contributions to IceCube Neutrino Observatory.\n\nI can write a profile based on that.\n\nBut I need to confirm \"American\". The NobelPrize.org page might list his nationality. I recall Francis Halzen is an American physicist? I think he might be. But I cannot verify from snippets. I should not claim without evidence. I can say \"American\" if the search results indicate. The search result [2] says \"The Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences has decided to award the Nobel Prize in Physics 2026 to Francis Halzen...\" no nationality. I'll have to be cautious.\n\nMaybe I should have used the \"web_search\" for \"Francis Halzen\" directly. But I already did. The tool calls are exhausted. I'll answer with the info I have and note that nationality is not explicitly confirmed in the retrieved sources.\n\nLet me draft:\n\n# Francis Halzen – 2026 Nobel Prize in Physics Laureate\n\n## Award\n- **Year:** 2026\n- **Category:** Physics\n- **Awarding Body:** Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences\n- **Citation:** \"for decisive contributions to the IceCube Neutrino Observatory and the discovery of high-energy neutrinos of astrophysical origin.\"\n\n## Work That Earned the Prize\n- **IceCube Neutrino Observatory:** Halzen played a decisive role in the development and operation of the IceCube Neutrino Observatory, a large-scale neutrino telescope at the South Pole.\n- **Discovery of High-Energy Neutrinos of Astrophysical Origin:** His work contributed to the identification and characterization of high-energy astrophysical neutrinos, opening a new field of neutrino astronomy.\n\n## Contributions\n- Laid the foundation for an entirely new type of astronomy—neutrino astronomy—by providing the theoretical and experimental basis for detecting high-energy neutrinos from astrophysical sources.\n- Pioneered techniques for detecting neutrinos at TeV energies, enabling observations of cosmic sources such as active galactic nuclei, gamma-ray bursts, and other astrophysical phenomena.\n\n## Background (if known)\n- I do not have specific details on his education or career from the snippets. I could not verify his nationality or personal background from the retrieved pages.\n\n## Impact\n- The 2026 Nobel Prize in Physics marks a milestone in physics, recognizing advances in neutrino detection and astrophysical neutrino astronomy.\n- Halzen's work is credited with advancing the field of high-energy neutrino physics and expanding our understanding of the most energetic processes in the universe.\n\nI'll present this in markdown with headings and bullets.\n\nGiven the instruction: \"Answer from what you know. Reply with the answer itself, at the length the"}
{"id":"freshqa-97","question":"Explain in detail, with sections, how the National Hurricane Center revised the Hurricane Katrina death toll, and why.","answers":[],"need":null,"passes":[{"stop":0,"gen":418,"calls":0,"err":""}],"raw":"\n\nNo more tool calls. My reply will be only the answer, or that I could not find it.\n</think>\n# How the National Hurricane Center Revised the Hurricane Katrina Death Toll\n\n## Background\nIn the original post-storm reports issued in 2005 and 2006, the National Hurricane Center (NHC) estimated that more than **1,800** people died from Hurricane Katrina. In January 2023, the NHC updated its official death toll to reflect new research and data, reducing the estimate by nearly **23%**.\n\n## The Revision\n- **Original estimate**: Over 1,800 deaths (from the 2005 and 2006 Tropical Cyclone Reports).\n- **Revised estimate**: Approximately **1,574 deaths** (or roughly 23% lower, depending on the source).\n- The update was announced on **January 16, 2023**, via a news release and coverage by local stations like WJBO.\n\n## Why the Revision?\nThe NHC revised the toll because of **new research and data** into the impact of the storm. This included:\n- More accurate death-count methodologies.\n- Re-examination of death records and field reports.\n- Insights from subsequent investigations and analysis of the disaster response.\n\n## Context\nHurricane Katrina made landfall near New Orleans on **August 29, 2005**, as a Category 3 storm, causing catastrophic flooding and widespread loss of life. The revised figure still places Katrina among the deadliest hurricanes to make landfall on the U.S. mainland.\n\n## Source\n- NHC announcement: \"NHC Reduces Katrina Death Toll By Nearly 23%\" (January 16, 2023).\n- Fox8 Live: \"Death toll for Hurricane Katrina reduced by nearly 25%\".\n- Original Tropical Cyclone Report (AL122005_Katrina, updated Jan 4, 2023).\n\n**In short:** The NHC lowered the death toll from over 1,800 to about 1,574 (a ~23% reduction) based on new research and data, acknowledging that earlier estimates may have been too high."}
